The anatomy:

```go
func divide(a, b int) (int, error)
//  func    keyword
//  divide  name (not part of the type)
//  a, b    parameter names (docs only — not the type)
//  int     the shared parameter type
//  (int, error)  the results
```

The signature AS A TYPE — what matters for assigning functions to variables, passing them, comparing:

```go
type MathFunc func(int, int) int

add := func(a, b int) int { return a + b }
var m MathFunc = add   // fits the signature type
m(3, 4)                // 7 — executed
```

func(int, int) int IS a type — a function type. Variable names in signatures are documentation (godoc shows them, callers read them), but two functions with the same types and different names are the SAME type.

The signature variants: single result can drop the parens (func f() int); multiple results need them (func f() (int, error)); zero results (func f()); variadic (func f(nums ...int) — the last parameter);
named results (func f() (result int, err error) — zero-initialized, naked-returnable, defer-mutable).

The comparison boundary: function types are comparable only to nil (func values compare false against nil, anything else is a compile error). They're not equatable values — two identical functions aren't == comparable (you compare by calling or by identity through pointers if you must).

The trap, demonstrated:

```go
funcs := make([]func() int, 3)
for i := 0; i < 3; i++ {
    funcs[i] = func() int { return i }
}
// pre-1.22: funcs[0](), funcs[1](), funcs[2]() → 3, 3, 3 (all the last value)
// 1.22+: → 0, 1, 2 (each iteration's own i) — executed
```

The pre-1.22 mechanics: the loop variable i was a single memory slot; every closure created in the loop captured THAT slot; when the closures ran (after the loop finished), the slot held 3. The goroutine variant was the classic production bug — a hundred goroutines all logging the last item.

The pre-1.22 fixes people wrote: pass it in (go func(v int) { ... }(i)) or shadow it (i := i inside the loop). Both work by giving each closure its own variable.
